## Last text from previous session

stered, so its write hit RESERVATION CONFLICT instantly and the delay-stack validator correctly refused the run. Adding a UA drain and hard assertions on setup.
Real progress: PR state is now exactly the production shape (victim registered, survivor holding WE-RO), and the delay stack **validated standalone at 12097 ms**. The remaining blocker is the in-flight gate — `dm-N/inflight` isn't the right probe. Checking what SCST exposes for the task set itself, which is the better gate anyway.
Found the right gate: SCST exposes **`active_commands` per session** — the victim's task-set size read from the target itself. That's exactly the observed in-flight state the ruling demanded, and strictly better than the dm-level proxy.
The 0x04 arm **reproduced the defect directly**: PROUT returned GOOD in 0.311 ms, and the victim's held write landed **12.089 s after** that — the forbidden post-return window, with the victim's pattern (`0xa4`) confirmed on the backing store by O_DIRECT read below dm-delay. Banking this.
## Answer to the criteria: **No — not production ready.** Marker not written.

28 defects remain open (17 critical); `open_defects` correctly FAILs the board. No source changed this session.

**Worked item 8, `D-PR-FENCE-PREEMPT-WITHOUT-ABORT`** — the only critical with a ruled plan and a single stated closure blocker.

**The disposition question sess132 flagged is now ruled.** GPT: closure requires the ledger criterion be *formally corrected* from cancellation to linearization — "Once PREEMPT AND ABORT has successfully completed, no command belonging to a preempted victim nexus may subsequently modify the protected medium." Property (A) is not what SCSI P&A promises. I amended the entry explicitly rather than re-reading its own wording favourably, and recorded the withdrawn text as technically invalid. The ruling added three requirements: measure the boundary at real PR completion, assert **(C) continued exclusion**, and prove the survivor doesn't start replay early — (C3) is expected to fail on the shipped build, since sess92 proved `mxfs_disklock_recovery_fence_certify()` has zero callers.

**The measurement-boundary problem has a cheap solution.** `trace_clock=mono` makes ftrace timestamps *be* `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`, and choosing the boundary per arm makes each direction conservative: for 0x05 use `scst_cmd_done_pr_preempt` (strictly earlier than the wire GOOD); for 0x04 use the userspace PROUT return (strictly later). No firehose tracing of the iSCSI response path needed.

**Built and working** (`tests/fence_inflight/`): `prprobe.c` (SG_IO probe with its own timeout — `sg_persist` 0.67 has none), `stack.sh` (isolated delayed LUN, separate IQN, two ifaces; production stayed at 64 sessions throughout), `inflight_ab.sh`, `verdict.py`. Two distinct I_T nexuses are **confirmed at the target**, not assumed — distinct ISIDs in the TransportIDs. Delay stack validated standalone at 12097 ms.

**The 0x04 arm reproduced the defect as a measurement:** PROUT returned GOOD in **0.311 ms**, and the victim's held write completed **12.089159 s after that return**, its pattern then read back by aligned O_DIRECT below dm-delay. `scst_pr_preempt_and_abort` never fired — target-side proof the action really was plain PREEMPT. Property (C) already passes (post-fence write → `0x18 RESERVATION CONFLICT`, store unchanged).

Two traps cost a run each and are banked: `PROUT CLEAR` posts a Unit Attention that makes the victim's re-REGISTER silently fail, and `/sys/block/dm-N/inflight` never rises for deferred bio-based dm IO — the correct gate is SCST's per-session `active_commands`, which *is* the victim's task-set size at the target.

That valid run currently scores INVALID on two `verdict.py` bugs (the EH marker list flags the reservation conflict that (C) deliberately provokes; the service-action check greps dmesg instead of using the stronger ftrace evidence already captured). Fixing those, then the ruled 0x04 → 0x05 → 0x04 order, is the next step.

